      "id": "10.1093/ismehm/aazag024",
      "title": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa lasR is a keystone gene in polymicrobial cultures",
      "abstract": "AbstractThe airways of people with cystic fibrosis (CF) are often co-infected by Pseudomonas aeruginosa and a variety of other co-habiting microbes; these infections are polymicrobial. P. aeruginosa isolates from the CF airways are also known to commonly acquire mutations in the quorum sensing regulator, lasR. In this work, we show that loss of lasR function has a profound impact on the stability of inter-species interactions in a polymicrobial ecosystem, and in particular, on the dynamics of a common CF-associated fungus, Candida albicans. Titres of C. albicans were stable in the presence of wild type P. aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus. However, when wild type P. aeruginosa was replaced by a ΔlasR mutant, C. albicans titres progressively declined over time. RNAseq analyses implicated a LasR-regulated Type VI Secretion System effector cluster (PA3904-PA3908) in this fungal instability, and indeed, ectopic expression of PA3904-PA3908 in the ΔlasR mutant reversed this decline in C. albicans titres. We also noted that challenge of the polymicrobial cultures with a clinically-relevant combination of colistin and ciprofloxacin led to a fungal bloom in the poly-cultures. This bloom was abolished in the ΔlasR mutant, but was restored by ectopic expression of the PA3904-PA3908 cluster of ORFs. Finally, we show that whereas wild type P. aeruginosa is relatively agnostic to the presence of other microbes, the ΔlasR mutant is not and undergoes substantial transcriptional reprogramming. Our data indicate that lasR has a large and previously unrecognised impact on inter-species interactions. We therefore propose that lasR is an ecological keystone gene.",
      "journal": "ISME Host Microbe",
      "doi": "10.1093/ismehm/aazag024",
