      "id": "10.1093/ismeco/ycag277",
      "title": "Macrofaunal bioturbation modulates microbial metabolic potential in arid mangrove sediments",
      "abstract": "AbstractBioturbation by benthic organisms substantially alters the physical and biogeochemical properties of mangrove sediments. Through burrowing and sediment reworking, bioturbators increase oxygen penetration into otherwise anoxic, waterlogged sediments and redistribute organic matter throughout the sediment profile. Although previous studies have shown that bioturbation modifies microbial community composition, its consequences for microbial functional potential remain poorly understood. Using metagenomics, we confirmed that crab bioturbation reshaped the composition of the sediment prokaryotic community and showed that this restructuring was accompanied by pronounced, depth-dependent shifts in metabolic potential. Gene- and pathway-level analyses indicated that bioturbation redirected bacterial and archaeal functional potential away from anaerobic degradation of complex organic matter and towards more metabolically versatile and oxidative processes, with the enrichment of functions associated with respiratory and central carbon metabolism, the utilisation of diverse carbon- and nitrogen-containing compounds, and traits potentially involved in nutrient mobilisation and plant growth promotion. Consistent with this functional-repertoire transition, diel gas-flux measurements revealed significantly higher CO2 emissions from bioturbated sediments, whereas CH4 emissions were not affected. We propose that these shifts enable sediment microbial communities to exploit newly available substrates, creating a more nutritionally favourable sediment milieu for both microbial communities and other sediment-dwellers, including mangrove trees. Because arid mangroves are typically constrained by patchy nutrient availability and persistent abiotic stress, including high salinity and aridity, these bioturbation-driven microbial functional shifts may play an important role in sustaining mangrove productivity and could represent a general mechanism linking ecosystem engineering to microbiome-mediated nutrient cycling across coastal sediments.",
