      "id": "10.1186/s40168-026-02570-y",
      "title": "Endophytic fungi shape tree epigenomes and enhance thermotolerance under climate warming",
      "abstract": "Background\n                Rising global temperatures threaten the survival and regeneration of temperate and boreal forests. Yet how microbial symbioses contribute to tree heat-resilience remains unclear.\n              \n                Results\n                Here, we integrated field ecological surveys with controlled multi-omics analyses and found that soil fungal diversity declined, accompanied by enrichment of potentially beneficial taxa along regional variation in max temperature of the warmest month (BIO5). Inoculation with representative endophytes significantly promoted birch (Betula platyphylla) growth and photosynthetic performance under non-stress conditions. Furthermore, endophytic fungi enhance thermal tolerance in birch by establishing a transcriptional chromatin state at heat-responsive genes that facilitates their activation under heat stress. Integrative network analyses identified regulatory networks linking fungal symbiosis to thermotolerance pathways.\n              \n                Conclusion\n                These findings connect macroecological patterns to molecular mechanisms, uncovering an epigenetic basis for microbe-mediated heat adaptation in trees and highlighting symbiotic fungi as both key partners and practical means to strengthen forest resilience under climate warming.\n                \n\n                  \n                    \n                      \n                        Video Abstract",
