      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1979616",
      "title": "Long-term starvation, nutrient-triggered resurgence, and the biogeographic distribution of marine bacterial taxa",
      "abstract": "In natural ecosystems, microorganisms face unpredictable environmental fluctuations, and nutrient starvation is a pervasive stressor. Surface waters of the Mariana Trench region are chronically oligotrophic, with microbial communities alternating between prolonged nutrient deprivation and episodic nutrient pulses. To identify bacterial taxa showing molecular persistence under prolonged starvation and relative resurgence after nutrient replenishment, we incubated Mariana Trench surface seawater in the dark under nutrient deprivation for 24 months and then amended the starved community with either dilute complex organic medium (1/100-strength 2216E) or a simple algal-derived substrate (Synechococcus sp. PCC7002 lysate). Six experimental units were used: two replicate barrels per substrate treatment, one sterile artificial seawater control, and one sterile artificial seawater barrel amended with PCC7002 lysate powder. Community responses were characterized by paired 16S rRNA gene (DNA) and 16S rRNA (cDNA) amplicon sequencing. No direct cell counting was performed, and all abundance values are relative read abundances rather than absolute cell abundances. After 24 months, dissolved organic carbon declined by ca. 10%, but community structure was profoundly reshuffled. Ten initially rare OTUs (each 1% after long-term incubation, consistent with the microbial seed bank concept. Nutrient amendment triggered resurgence of 29 OTUs, which partitioned into three response types with clear treatment-specific preferences. Elevated 16S rRNA:16S rRNA gene ratios indicated enhanced transcriptional activity of resurging populations, with the first detectable response at one day post amendment. Cross-referencing against 39 Tara Oceans surface stations showed that 75% of analyzable starvation-tolerant genera and 82% of analyzable resurgent genera occurred at ≥30 stations across the Pacific, Atlantic, Indian and Southern Oceans. These results suggest that starvation tolerance and nutrient responsiveness are ecological traits associated with broad biogeographic occurrence, supporting a plausible link between microbial life-history strategies and large-scale biogeographic patterns in the oligotrophic ocean, although our correlational data cannot establish causality.",
