      "id": "10.1111/1758-2229.70471",
      "title": "Beyond Inhibition: From Growth Arrest to Gene Regulation in Agrobacterium radiobacter Exposed to Monoterpenes",
      "abstract": "Effects of monoterpene exposure on Agrobacterium radiobacter.\n\nABSTRACT\nMonoterpenes, major constituents of essential oils, exhibit broad‐spectrum antimicrobial activity and represent promising candidates for sustainable crop protection. Here, we investigated the responses of the biocontrol bacterium Agrobacterium radiobacter to seven structurally distinct monoterpenes using an integrated approach combining antimicrobial assays, real‐time growth monitoring, matrix‐assisted laser desorption/ionisation time‐of‐flight mass spectrometry (MALDI‐TOF MS) spectral profiling, and gene expression analysis. D‐carvone exhibited the highest antibacterial efficacy (MIC50 = 0.318 mg/mL), whereas β‐pinene and bornyl acetate were the least active. Real‐time growth monitoring revealed pronounced time‐dependent inhibition, with strong early suppression during the first 0–3 h followed by partial growth recovery. MALDI‐TOF MS analysis demonstrated structure‐specific alterations in bacterial protein spectra, with (S)‐(–)‐limonene and bornyl acetate inducing the most pronounced changes, while β‐pinene and sabinene showed comparatively minor effects. Hierarchical cluster analysis identified 3 h as the most discriminative time point for detecting monoterpene‐induced responses. Gene expression analysis revealed compound‐specific regulation of exoY, hrcS, ftsI, achI and ncpAch, with the strongest transcriptional changes associated with α‐pinene, D‐carvone and 3‐carene. Collectively, discrepancies observed across antimicrobial activity, growth kinetics, protein spectral profiles, and gene expression indicate that monoterpenes do not operate through a single mechanism, but rather elicit multiple, structure‐dependent modes of action. These findings provide mechanistic insight into plant metabolite–rhizosphere bacteria interactions and highlight the importance of integrated approaches for evaluating antimicrobial activity in biological control systems.\n\nEffects of monoterpene exposure on Agrobacterium radiobacter.\n\nABSTRACT\nMonoterpenes, major constituents of essential oils, exhibit broad-spectrum antimicrobial activity and represent promising candidates for sustainable crop protection. Here, we investigated the responses of the biocontrol bacterium Agrobacterium radiobacter to seven structurally distinct monoterpenes using an integrated approach combining antimicrobial assays, real-time growth monitoring, matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionisation time-of-flight mass spectrometry (MALDI-TOF MS) spectral profiling, and gene expression analysis. D-carvone exhibited the highest antibacterial efficacy (MIC50 = 0.318 mg/mL), whereas β-pinene and bornyl acetate were the least active. Real-time growth monitoring revealed pronounced time-dependent inhibition, with strong early suppression during the first 0–3 h followed by partial growth recovery. MALDI-TOF MS analysis demonstrated structure-specific alterations in bacterial protein spectra, with (S)-(–)-limonene and bornyl acetate inducing the most pronounced changes, while β-pinene and sabinene showed comparatively minor effects. Hierarchical cluster analysis identified 3 h as the most discriminative time point for detecting monoterpene-induced responses. Gene expression analysis revealed compound-specific regulation of exoY, hrcS, ftsI, achI and ncpAch, with the strongest transcriptional changes associated with α-pinene, D-carvone and 3-carene. Collectively, discrepancies observed across antimicrobial activity, growth kinetics, protein spectral profiles, and gene expression indicate that monoterpenes do not operate through a single mechanism, but rather elicit multiple, structure-dependent modes of action. These findings provide mechanistic insight into plant metabolite–rhizosphere bacteria interactions and highlight the importance of integrated approaches for evaluating antimicrobial activity in biological control systems.",
