      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1950141",
      "title": "Marburg virus in bats and humans: ecology, immunity, pathogenesis, and diagnostic challenges",
      "abstract": "Over the past few decades, viral hemorrhagic fevers (VHFs) have caused several outbreaks of international public health concern. Among them, diseases caused by filoviruses, including Marburg virus (MARV) and Ebola virus (EBOV), represent a major challenge for endemic African countries because of their capacity to sustain human-to-human transmission and the limited availability of appropriate treatments and facilities for outbreak containment. MARV is a zoonotic pathogen, and the Egyptian fruit bat, Rousettus aegyptiacus, has been identified as its natural reservoir. One of the main features of bats as viral reservoirs is their ability to sustain infection without developing overt disease. Moreover, a harmonized approach to study the distribution and dynamics of MARV in the bat populations would be required to better assess the risk of future spillover events. In contrast, Marburg virus disease (MVD) in humans is associated with severe hemorrhagic syndrome and case fatality rates of up to 88%. Differences in immune regulation and activation between bat and human hosts are thought to contribute to these contrasting clinical outcomes. Current evidence points to distinctive features of bat antiviral immunity, including an expanded type I interferon repertoire, early and transient antiviral responses, and tightly regulated inflammatory activation, which may contribute to viral control while limiting infection-associated tissue damage. In this scenario, rapid and specific diagnosis of suspected cases is essential for appropriate patient management, outbreak control, and assessment of transmission risk. Currently, validated in vitro diagnostic (IVD) assays for the laboratory confirmation of MARV infection are based on real-time RT PCR, whereas all available antigenic and serological assays remain restricted to research use. Recent advances in molecular diagnostics have enabled the development of multiplex assays for the simultaneous detection of several pathogens, as well as isothermal amplification platforms that can be deployed outside conventional laboratory settings. Nevertheless, serological analysis and genome sequencing remain valuable for the immunological and epidemiological characterization of MARV.",
