      "id": "10.1111/1751-7915.70465",
      "title": "Constraint‐Based Combinatorial Screening Identifies a Single Bacterial Partner That Restores Microalgal Growth in an Inhibitory Waste Feedstock",
      "abstract": "Constraint‐based screening of 1159 algal‐bacterial consortia revealed rare, strain‐specific partnerships that rescue algal growth under inhibitory waste conditions, particularly with Flagellimonas sp. (No. 18). These findings establish a scalable framework to identify functional consortia for waste valorisation.\n\nABSTRACT\nMicroalgae–bacteria interactions provide a route to engineer growth behaviour under chemically constrained environments, yet effective partnerships remain difficult to predict from monoculture traits alone. Here, we use industrial compost leachate as a defined inhibitory background to render growth rescue a measurable signature of functional interaction. Under 20% leachate, both axenic Nannochloropsis oculata and most co‐cultures with bacterial isolates exhibited strong growth suppression. A small number of defined algal–bacterial consortia converted this suppressed state into sustained algal growth, and the effect was strain‐dependent rather than a general consequence of bacterial presence. A combinatorial screen comprising 1159 consortia was evaluated using a differential, rate‐based growth score derived from algal specific growth rate. Growth rescue was rare and directional. High‐performance outcomes were concentrated in consortia containing Flagellimonas sp., and the single‐partner No. 18 condition itself produced strong rescue. Flask‐scale cultivation of selected exemplars using optical density (740 nm) preserved the tier‐level separation observed in fluorescence‐based screening, supporting cross‐format concordance of the screening outcome. Microscopy of selected exemplar consortia additionally revealed cellulose‐associated structures and intracellular lipid‐associated inclusions within the harvested biomass. As their chemical composition was not resolved, these inclusions are reported as a secondary phenotypic observation without assignment to polyhydroxyalkanoates or other specific storage polymers. Together, these results show that constraint‐based screening can identify algal–bacterial partnerships that restore growth under an inhibitory waste‐stream and trace the observed effect to defined microbial members.\n\nConstraint-based screening of 1159 algal-bacterial consortia revealed rare, strain-specific partnerships that rescue algal growth under inhibitory waste conditions, particularly with Flagellimonas sp. (No. 18). These findings establish a scalable framework to identify functional consortia for waste valorisation.\n\nABSTRACT\nMicroalgae–bacteria interactions provide a route to engineer growth behaviour under chemically constrained environments, yet effective partnerships remain difficult to predict from monoculture traits alone. Here, we use industrial compost leachate as a defined inhibitory background to render growth rescue a measurable signature of functional interaction. Under 20% leachate, both axenic Nannochloropsis oculata and most co-cultures with bacterial isolates exhibited strong growth suppression. A small number of defined algal–bacterial consortia converted this suppressed state into sustained algal growth, and the effect was strain-dependent rather than a general consequence of bacterial presence. A combinatorial screen comprising 1159 consortia was evaluated using a differential, rate-based growth score derived from algal specific growth rate. Growth rescue was rare and directional. High-performance outcomes were concentrated in consortia containing Flagellimonas sp., and the single-partner No. 18 condition itself produced strong rescue. Flask-scale cultivation of selected exemplars using optical density (740 nm) preserved the tier-level separation observed in fluorescence-based screening, supporting cross-format concordance of the screening outcome. Microscopy of selected exemplar consortia additionally revealed cellulose-associated structures and intracellular lipid-associated inclusions within the harvested biomass. As their chemical composition was not resolved, these inclusions are reported as a secondary phenotypic observation without assignment to polyhydroxyalkanoates or other specific storage polymers. Together, these results show that constraint-based screening can identify algal–bacterial partnerships that restore growth under an inhibitory waste-stream and trace the observed effect to defined microbial members.",
