      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1962118",
      "title": "Trichoderma ghanense N28-driven reprogramming of transcriptional and metabolic networks enhances vegetative growth and salinity tolerance in Reaumuria soongorica seedlings",
      "abstract": "Soil salinization is a global ecological problem that seriously restricts vegetation restoration and the stability of desert ecosystems in arid and semi-arid regions. Plant growth-promoting fungi (PGPF) play an important role in improving saline–alkali soils and enhancing the salt tolerance of plants. Reaumuria soongorica is a dominant constructive shrub in the salinized deserts of northwest China. However, the exploration of rhizosphere PGPF associated with R. soongorica, together with the functional verification of these fungi and the regulatory mechanisms by which they mediate salt tolerance, has been rarely reported. In this study, the strain Trichoderma ghanense N28 was isolated from the rhizosphere soil of R. soongorica. A pot experiment under 400 mM NaCl treatment simulating salt-stressed habitat conditions was conducted in combination with multi-omics approaches to systematically elucidate the regulatory effects and molecular mechanisms of N28 on the growth and salt tolerance of R. soongorica seedlings under salt stress. Inoculation with N28 effectively alleviated salt-induced damage and promoted seedling growth by coordinately regulating ion balance, photosynthetic performance, osmotic adjustment, and antioxidant defense, thereby mitigating oxidative stress. N28 enhanced the salt tolerance of R. soongorica and promoted biomass accumulation through the coordinated regulation of plant antioxidant pathways, secondary metabolism, and the photosynthetic system. Verified in pot experiments, this strain is a promising candidate inoculant for seedling propagation and nursery cultivation of R. soongorica, providing a feasible microbial inoculation scheme for improving the salt tolerance of desert plant seedlings at the seedling stage, and offering a strain reserve and technical support for subsequent inoculation trials under natural vegetation restoration scenarios.",
