      "id": "10.1186/s42523-026-00657-3",
      "title": "The gut microbiota and metabolomic profiles in cutaneous and renal glomerular vasculopathy-affected dogs reveal a pattern consistent with early-onset starvation",
      "abstract": "Background\n                     Canine Cutaneous and Renal Glomerular Vasculopathy (CRGV) is a frequently fatal, idiopathic disease characterised by cutaneous lesions and vasculopathy of the glomerular vessels associated with thrombotic microangiopathy and acute kidney injury. Studies have been unable to identify the aetiology of CRGV; however, multiple bacterial causes have been hypothesised with limited evidence. Here, we characterised the faecal microbiota and metabolome of CRGV-affected (n = 85) and medically healthy (MH; n = 96) dogs using 16S rRNA gene sequencing and proton nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy (1H NMR).\n                  \n                     Results\n                     Alpha diversity (Shannon entropy, Chao1 richness, observed features) differed significantly between MH and CRGV-affected dogs (p < 0.05 Benjamini–Hochberg (BH) false discovery rate (FDR) -adjusted). A non-metric multidimensional scaling (NMDS) analysis using Bray–Curtis dissimilarity based on family-level abundance (PERMANOVA p = 0.005 BH FDR-adjusted; PERMDISP p = 0.001 BH FDR-adjusted) showed distinct clustering between cohorts. Seven bacterial families (Anaeroplasmataceae, Bacteroidaceae, Clostridiaceae, Enterobacteriaceae, Enterococcaceae, Peptostreptococcaceae, and Ruminococcaceae) were most strongly associated with variation in community structure in the NMDS ordination. Analysis of Compositions of Microbiomes with Bias Correction 2 (ANCOM-BC2), adjusted for metadata variables to account for potential confounding factors (e.g., breed class size), identified seven bacterial families that differed significantly between cohorts (q < 0.05 BH FDR-adjusted); four of these (Enterobacteriaceae, Enterococcaceae, Prevotellaceae, and Veillonellaceae) remained significant in pseudo-count sensitivity analysis. The remaining three (Coriobacteriaceae, Peptostreptococcaceae, and unclassified Clostridia) were not robust to pseudo-count addition. Relative to MH samples, CRGV-affected samples exhibited higher abundance of Enterobacteriaceae and Enterococcaceae, and lower abundance of Prevotellaceae and Veillonellaceae. Metabolomic analysis further revealed differences in several metabolites consistent with a starvation-like metabolic profile (alanine, choline, methylsuccinic acid, and valine).\n                  \n                     Conclusion\n                     Taken together, the elevated amino acids, including branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs), alongside increases in Enterobacteriaceae and Enterococcaceae in CRGV-affected dogs, are consistent with metabolic alterations that resemble profiles reported in humans and murine models. While inappetence is clinically expected in these animals, the combined microbiota–metabolite profile is consistent with a starvation-like metabolic profile, offering new insight into the metabolic profiles associated with CRGV and opening potential avenues to explore nutritional interventions as a supportive treatment for CRGV.\n                  \n                     Graphical Abstract",
