      "id": "10.1111/1462-2920.70476",
      "title": "Distance‐ and Direction‐Dependent Effects of Macroalgae on Early Epilithic Bacterial Assemblages and Coral Settlement Processes",
      "abstract": "By conditioning tiles in situ, this study showed that macroalgae shape epilithic bacterial assemblages, with strong effects 1 cm downstream. Settlement of coral larvae coincided with distance‐ and flow‐dependent bacterial shifts and responded to specific bacterial taxa. Recruit survival was consistently reduced near and downstream of Dictyota bartayresiana. This study demonstrates how macroalgae can structure coral settlement at the centimetre‐scales, a process likely mediated by epilithic bacterial assemblages.\n\nABSTRACT\nWhile the expansion of macroalgae on coral reefs is a global concern, the spatial scales and mechanisms through which macroalgae inhibit coral recruitment processes and the role of epilithic bacteria in coral settlement remain inadequately understood. Using tiles preconditioned at three distances of two algal species in both up and down‐current directions, we show that macroalgae shape early epilithic bacterial assemblages, with strong effects 1 cm downstream of both algae. When exposing the tiles to larvae of two coral species in ex situ settlement experiments, the coral Acropora pulchra showed reduced larval settlement on cryptic surfaces of tiles conditioned 1 cm up and downstream of the alga Dictyota bartayresiana, whereas the coral Pocillopora acuta showed the opposite pattern. Specific bacterial taxa within the families Bdellovibrionaceae, Pirellulaceae, Flavobacteriaceae and Rhodobacteraceae were associated with P. acuta settlement with differences across algal species. When tiles with coral spat were returned to their initial positions on the reef, spat survival was consistently reduced closer to, and particularly 1 cm downstream of, D. bartayresiana. These results demonstrate that macroalgae influence coral settlement and post‐settlement survival at centimetre scales through distance‐ and flow‐dependent effects and indicate that these effects may be mediated by epilithic bacterial assemblages.\n\nBy conditioning tiles in situ, this study showed that macroalgae shape epilithic bacterial assemblages, with strong effects 1 cm downstream. Settlement of coral larvae coincided with distance- and flow-dependent bacterial shifts and responded to specific bacterial taxa. Recruit survival was consistently reduced near and downstream of Dictyota bartayresiana. This study demonstrates how macroalgae can structure coral settlement at the centimetre-scales, a process likely mediated by epilithic bacterial assemblages.\n\nABSTRACT\nWhile the expansion of macroalgae on coral reefs is a global concern, the spatial scales and mechanisms through which macroalgae inhibit coral recruitment processes and the role of epilithic bacteria in coral settlement remain inadequately understood. Using tiles preconditioned at three distances of two algal species in both up and down-current directions, we show that macroalgae shape early epilithic bacterial assemblages, with strong effects 1 cm downstream of both algae. When exposing the tiles to larvae of two coral species in ex situ settlement experiments, the coral Acropora pulchra showed reduced larval settlement on cryptic surfaces of tiles conditioned 1 cm up and downstream of the alga Dictyota bartayresiana, whereas the coral Pocillopora acuta showed the opposite pattern. Specific bacterial taxa within the families Bdellovibrionaceae, Pirellulaceae, Flavobacteriaceae and Rhodobacteraceae were associated with P. acuta settlement with differences across algal species. When tiles with coral spat were returned to their initial positions on the reef, spat survival was consistently reduced closer to, and particularly 1 cm downstream of, D. bartayresiana. These results demonstrate that macroalgae influence coral settlement and post-settlement survival at centimetre scales through distance- and flow-dependent effects and indicate that these effects may be mediated by epilithic bacterial assemblages.",
