      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1982871",
      "title": "Differences in the oral microbiota of long-term liver transplant recipients: community structure and predicted functional potential",
      "abstract": "BackgroundLong-term liver transplant recipients are exposed to sustained immunosuppression and remain vulnerable to infectious and inflammatory complications. The oral microbiota may reflect changes in host immune status and systemic microecological balance, and may also participate in extra-oral inflammation through oral–systemic pathways. However, the oral microbial features of long-term surviving liver transplant recipients, particularly those surviving for more than 5 years after transplantation, remain unclear. This study aimed to characterize the distribution patterns, microbial phenotypes, and potential functional alterations of the oral microbiota in long-term liver transplant recipients.Materials and methodsSaliva samples were obtained from 20 long-term liver transplant recipients (Ltx) and 20 healthy controls (HC). Oral microbial profiles were analyzed by 16S rRNA gene sequencing. Microbial diversity, community structure, differential taxa, predicted phenotypes, and functional pathways were assessed using alpha and beta diversity analyses, linear discriminant analysis effect size (LEfSe), BugBase, PICRUSt2, and KEGG pathway enrichment analysis.ResultsAlpha diversity did not differ significantly between Ltx and HC. In contrast, weighted UniFrac-based principal coordinates analysis (PCoA) and PERMANOVA analysis revealed significant differences in community structure. LEfSe analysis showed enrichment of Neisseria and Fusobacterium and a reduction of Leptotrichia in liver transplant recipients. Phenotype prediction suggested increased aerobic, stress-tolerant, and biofilm-forming features, together with reduced anaerobic features, in the Ltx group. Functional prediction indicated that pathways related to energy metabolism, oxidative stress response, and bacterial secretion systems were more abundant, whereas pathways associated with translation, nucleotide metabolism, cell wall biosynthesis, porphyrin metabolism, and amino acid biosynthesis were reduced.ConclusionIn this exploratory cross-sectional study, long-term liver transplant recipients exhibited differences in oral microbial community composition and predicted functional potential compared with healthy controls, despite similar alpha-diversity indices. The observed differences in predicted traits related to aerobic adaptation, stress tolerance, biofilm formation, and energy metabolism may indicate alterations in the functional potential of the oral microbiota. These findings provide insights into the oral microbial characteristics of long-term liver transplant recipients and may inform future research on oral health management after transplantation.",
