      "id": "10.1111/1751-7915.70484",
      "title": "Academic Philanthropy: Advancing Education and Societal Advancement by Mobilising the Untapped Intellectual Synthetic Capital of Silver Academia",
      "abstract": "Contributions of academic philanthropy.\n\nABSTRACT\nThe quest for new knowledge, and knowledge dissemination and application, are central to the functioning and advancement of society. However, despite significant investment by state and philanthropic actors, educational provision remains insufficient to satisfy global demand for education and educators. Education also represents a major form of inequality among peoples. Philanthropic actors may be both wealthy individuals and organisations donating financial assets, and voluntary actors contributing non‐financial assets, such as time, effort, expertise or emotional support. Here we discuss the need to enable and promote academic philanthropy based on the voluntary transfer of knowledge, experience, expertise and synthetic capacity—resources that, unlike material wealth, represent non‐rival public goods that are not diluted but instead grow in value through sharing. Educators and academics are society's professional agents for the acquisition and dissemination of knowledge, its critical interpretation and understanding, and its application. However, mandatory retirement policies in some settings bring these contributions to an abrupt end, resulting in an unnecessary loss of accumulated intellectual capital from the education ecosystem and reduction in potential return on society's long‐term investment in education and research. We argue for a more flexible and adapted retirement framework in which willing and able retired academics—silver academics—may continue contributing their knowledge, expertise and experience to society beyond statutory retirement age. It is essential that such service complements rather than competes with the work of career academics—those in regular employment—by focusing on supportive activities such as mentoring, strategic guidance, serving as a source of institutional memory, and selected educational and administrative tasks. The support of career academics by retirees, and stimulation of retirees by career academics, could create a virtuous cycle of intergenerational intellectual exchange and synthesis. Importantly, unlike wealthy financial philanthropists who are relatively few in number, potential academic philanthropists are very many. Academic philanthropy represents a practical, low cost and readily implementable mechanism for strengthening education ecosystems, reducing widespread inequalities in access to knowledge and increasing return on societal investment in educational capital.\n\nContributions of academic philanthropy.\n\nABSTRACT\nThe quest for new knowledge, and knowledge dissemination and application, are central to the functioning and advancement of society. However, despite significant investment by state and philanthropic actors, educational provision remains insufficient to satisfy global demand for education and educators. Education also represents a major form of inequality among peoples. Philanthropic actors may be both wealthy individuals and organisations donating financial assets, and voluntary actors contributing non-financial assets, such as time, effort, expertise or emotional support. Here we discuss the need to enable and promote academic philanthropy based on the voluntary transfer of knowledge, experience, expertise and synthetic capacity—resources that, unlike material wealth, represent non-rival public goods that are not diluted but instead grow in value through sharing. Educators and academics are society's professional agents for the acquisition and dissemination of knowledge, its critical interpretation and understanding, and its application. However, mandatory retirement policies in some settings bring these contributions to an abrupt end, resulting in an unnecessary loss of accumulated intellectual capital from the education ecosystem and reduction in potential return on society's long-term investment in education and research. We argue for a more flexible and adapted retirement framework in which willing and able retired academics—silver academics—may continue contributing their knowledge, expertise and experience to society beyond statutory retirement age. It is essential that such service complements rather than competes with the work of career academics—those in regular employment—by focusing on supportive activities such as mentoring, strategic guidance, serving as a source of institutional memory, and selected educational and administrative tasks. The support of career academics by retirees, and stimulation of retirees by career academics, could create a virtuous cycle of intergenerational intellectual exchange and synthesis. Importantly, unlike wealthy financial philanthropists who are relatively few in number, potential academic philanthropists are very many. Academic philanthropy represents a practical, low cost and readily implementable mechanism for strengthening education ecosystems, reducing widespread inequalities in access to knowledge and increasing return on societal investment in educational capital.",
