      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1977219",
      "title": "FeoAB disruption alters intracellular iron homeostasis without reversing PmrAB mutation-driven colistin resistance in Salmonella Typhimurium",
      "abstract": "IntroductionPmrAB activation is an important chromosomal mechanism of colistin resistance in Salmonella enterica and can be accompanied by changes in bacterial iron homeostasis. However, whether FeoAB-mediated ferrous iron uptake is required to maintain an established PmrAB mutation-mediated resistant state remains unclear.MethodsIn this study, previously characterized colistin-resistant PmrAL105P and PmrBR93P strains derived from Salmonella enterica serovar Typhimurium ATCC 14028 were used to examine the contribution of FeoAB to resistance maintenance. The feoA and feoB genes were individually deleted, and colistin susceptibility, feoAB expression, intracellular iron, expression of lipid A modification-associated genes, transcription of the O-antigen chain-length regulators fepE and wzzST, lipid A profiles, cell-associated colistin binding, and outer membrane permeability were evaluated.ResultsBoth PmrAB-mutant strains showed elevated feoA and feoB transcription, ranging from 2.58- to 3.36-fold relative to WT14. Intracellular Fe2+ increased from 0.68 nmol/mg protein in WT14 to 1.01 and 1.16 nmol/mg protein in the PmrAL105P and PmrBR93P mutants, respectively. Deletion of feoA or feoB reduced intracellular Fe2+ to 0.53–0.71 nmol/mg protein, whereas the colistin MIC remained unchanged at 8 μg/mL in all resistant derivatives, including under the Chelex-treated medium conditions. Expression of pmrE, pmrH, and pmrK remained elevated after FeoAB disruption, whereas transcription of the O-antigen chain-length regulators fepE and wzzST showed only modest variation. Lipid A analysis showed that characteristic L-Ara4N-associated ions at m/z 1927.2 and 2165.2 remained detectable after feoA or feoB deletion. Reduced cell-associated colistin binding and lower NPN uptake were also retained.DiscussionThese findings indicate that FeoAB contributes to the altered intracellular iron homeostasis associated with PmrAB-mutant strains but is dispensable for maintaining the MIC-defined colistin-resistant phenotype under the tested conditions. The persistence of the PmrA-regulated L-Ara4N pathway after FeoAB disruption supports the conclusion that FeoAB-associated iron remodeling is not sufficient as a single determinant for reversing this resistance-proximal pathway.",
