      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1964478",
      "title": "Oat β-glucan modulates the cecal microbiota and fatty acid profile, providing insights into gut microbiota-mediated growth promotion in weaned rabbits",
      "abstract": "While our previous work demonstrated that dietary oat β-glucan improves growth performance (increased BW, ADG and ADFI) and mitigates systemic inflammation (reduced serum TNF-α, IL-1β and LPS) in weaned rabbits via colonic modulation, the upstream mechanisms within the cecum, which is the primary fermentation site, remained unexplored. To bridge this gap using data from the same experimental cohort, the present study specifically examines the cecal ecosystem and its contribution to these phenotypic benefits. Twenty-four three-week-old weaned female New Zealand White rabbits were randomly allocated to a control group (CT) and an oat β-glucan supplementation group (BG, 200 mg/kg body weight) for a 4-week trial. Although supplementation induced no significant changes in cecal morphology, the BG group showed significantly elevated concentrations of propionic acid, valeric acid, hexanoic acid and total short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) in the cecum (p < 0.05), with butyric acid exhibiting an upward trend (p = 0.052). Although oat β-glucan supplementation did not significantly alter cecal microbiota α or β diversity, it induced specific compositional shifts, most notably a significant increase in the phylum Proteobacteria and the enrichment of certain bacterial genera, particularly Odoribacter. Notably, Odoribacter was positively associated with butyric acid (q = 0.024), and Proteobacteria with propionic acid (q = 0.006). Furthermore, Proteobacteria was negatively associated with LPS (q = 0.023), Odoribacter with TNF-α (q = 0.045), and Blastococcus with ADG (q = 0.031). These metabolic and microbial shifts in the cecum were consistent with a scenario in which they contribute to the aforementioned improvements in performance and inflammation, paralleling the alterations previously identified in the colon and suggesting a coordinated hindgut response to oat β-glucan supplementation. Together, these findings extend the characterization of colonic microbiota alterations induced by oat β-glucan to the cecum and outline an associative framework for its potential contribution to the growth and anti-inflammatory effects observed in this cohort.",
