      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.1953988",
      "title": "Multi-omics dissection of azo dye degradation in Shewanella xiamenensis LC6 reveals transcriptional and proteomic reprogramming during methyl orange decolorization",
      "abstract": "Synthetic azo dyes represent major environmental pollutants due to their widespread industrial use, chemical stability, and resistance to conventional wastewater treatment. Shewanella xiamenensis LC6, a bacterium isolated from textile wastewater, has demonstrated remarkable azo dye decolorization capacity, yet the molecular mechanisms underlying this process remain poorly understood. We employed a multi-omics approach combining genomics, transcriptomics (RNA-seq), proteomics (DIA-MS), and quantitative Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (1H-qNMR) to investigate the molecular response of S. xiamenensis LC6 during methyl orange (MO) decolorization. The S. xiamenensis LC6 genome was sequenced using PacBio and Illumina platforms, and differential expression analyses were performed at 20%, 50%, and 90% decolorization stages. Under static, low oxygen conditions, S. xiamenensis LC6 decolorized 98.95% of MO in 12 h and exhibited broad dye-degrading capacity against various azo and anthraquinone dyes. Genome analysis revealed two megaplasmids carrying metal resistance and conjugation genes. Transcriptomic and proteomic profiling identified massive overexpression of FMN-dependent NADH:quinone oxidoreductase (azoR; log₂FC 6.05 transcriptionally, 4.15 and 5.11 proteomically), a protein lacking a signal peptide and transmembrane domains that likely acts as an intracellular quinone reductase rather than a canonical azoreductase. Notably, genes and proteins involved in iron and heme uptake and storage, including TonB-dependent receptors, HutX/HutZ heme utilization proteins and ferritin, were strongly upregulated, indicating a perturbation of iron/heme homeostasis during MO decolorization. The 1H-qNMR analysis confirmed MO azo-bond cleavage and sulfanilic acid accumulation. In conclusion, S. xiamenensis LC6 employs a coordinated multi-layered response to MO decolorization involving azo-bond cleavage, stress adaptation, iron homeostasis, and the induction of dioxygenases and efflux-associated proteins. These findings highlight S. xiamenensis LC6 as a promising candidate for textile effluent bioremediation and provide molecular targets for strain engineering.",
