      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.2029479",
      "title": "Rhizosphere bacterial and fungal community responses to long-term tillage and manure addition in rainfed winter wheat",
      "abstract": "Long-term tillage and manure addition can alter the rhizosphere microbial communities, but the responses of bacteria and fungi may differ. In a rainfed winter wheat experiment established in 2007 in Shanxi, China, we compared rhizosphere microbial communities under conventional tillage (CT), minimum tillage (MT), with and without manure addition (CT + M and MT + M). Using 16S rRNA gene and internal transcribed spacer (ITS) amplicon sequencing, we assessed microbial diversity, taxonomic composition, co-occurrence networks, and their associations with soil properties. Manure addition increased bacterial richness, whereas fungal richness was affected by manure addition and its interaction with tillage. Neither bacterial nor fungal Shannon index differed significantly among treatments. Tillage significantly affected overall fungal community composition, whereas none of the treatment factors significantly affected overall bacterial community composition. Among dominant taxa, manure addition affected certain bacterial groups, whereas tillage affected several fungal groups. Compared with the CT treatment, other treatments exhibited fewer network links, lower network density, and higher modularity. The MT + M treatment had the highest soil organic carbon, available phosphorus, and available potassium contents. These findings highlight distinct responses of bacterial and fungal communities to long-term tillage and manure management and their associations with soil properties in rainfed wheat systems.",
