      "id": "10.3389/fmicb.2026.2019924",
      "title": "A juvenile BALB/c mouse model for short-window, multi-tissue quantitative pharmacodynamic assessment of antiviral activity against contemporary clade IIb monkeypox virus",
      "abstract": "Monkeypox virus (MPV) clade IIb continues to circulate following the 2022 global outbreak, underscoring the need for practical small-animal systems in which antivirals can be evaluated quantitatively against contemporary isolates. Here, we evaluated a juvenile BALB/c mouse model infected with the 2022 clade IIb clinical isolate WIBP-MPXV-001. Three tissues (ovary, lung, and spleen) and three quantitative readouts (infectious virus titer, viral DNA load, and organ index) were assessed within a defined 5-day window. A parallel cohort monitored through day 14 tested whether that window captures measurable viral burden. Female weanling BALB/c mice were intraperitoneally inoculated with 107 PFU of MPV and developed a consistent systemic disease phenotype by day 5, characterized by ruffled fur and reduced activity. Severe bilateral ovarian enlargement was accompanied by high ovarian infectious virus burdens (median 6.74 log10 PFU/g). Three cidofovir (CDV; 100 mg/kg, s.c.) regimens were evaluated: prophylaxis at −1 dpi, a single dose at 0 dpi (2 h post-challenge), and two doses at 0 + 3 dpi (first dose 2 h post-challenge). All three prevented the clinical signs observed in untreated animals and significantly reduced ovarian infectious titers. Ovarian indices were reduced under all three regimens. Infectious virus was detectable in the lung and spleen of untreated animals but fell below the assay detection limit in all treated animals. Lung viral DNA loads were also significantly reduced, whereas the splenic reduction was not significant and the ovarian reduction reached significance only with prophylaxis. In the 14-day cohort, one untreated mouse died on day 6, and recoverable infectious virus fell below the assay detection limit in all surviving animals while viral DNA remained detectable. The 5-day window therefore captures a period during which antiviral effects can be compared quantitatively. These findings support the juvenile BALB/c model as a complementary approach for short-window, multi-tissue pharmacodynamic evaluation of antiviral activity against contemporary clade IIb MPV.",
